==Latin==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|cōdex}}

===Etymology===
{{unc|la}}. Most likely to be connected to {{m|la|cūdō||I beat, strike}}, both deriving from the same dental extension of {{der|la|ine-pro|*kewh₂-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*keh₂w-||to beat, hew, chop}},<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=caudex, -icis|page=99}}</ref> and so originally meant “that which has been cleaved off”. See also {{m|la|cauda||tail}}. Another possibility is a relation to {{m|la|caulis||stalk}}, if this is an ''l''-stem derivative of the same ultimate root, perhaps {{m|ine-pro|*ḱewh₁-|*ḱawh₁-|to swell; hollow}} (whence {{m|la|cavus}}) if both words originally meant “hollow stem”.

An old theory connected it to {{m+|ine-pro|*Heh₃s-||ash tree}}, the same source as {{cog|en|ash}} and many other synonymous cognates across {{cog|ine}} languages, stemming from the assumption that Indo-Europeans used hollowed out ash trees as boats and skiffs.<ref>{{R:ine:Schrader:1890}}</ref> However, this would be phonetically difficult if not impossible through an [[inherited]] route; otherwise it may have come to Latin through a {{der|la|qfa-sub}} intermediate.

